    "voice": {
      "script": "How does Python actually talk to you? Every program needs a way to receive information and a way to give it back. Python has two built-ins for exactly that. The input function pauses your program and waits for the user to type something. Whatever they type comes back as text, and we store it in a variable called name. Then the print function takes that value and displays it. So the data flows in one direction. Input brings it in, the variable holds it, print sends it back out. Remember: input gets information in, print sends information out. Now you try. Write a program that asks for your age and prints it back.",
